### Ordered checks after R7

- Point 15, dimension merge and large-BLOCK accumulation: not hit. Each row is
  one independent stats unit with one full-coverage N=2048 vector load/reduce;
  there is no stats-unit nested loop, low-coverage mask, or scalar accumulation.
- Point 16, continuous copy aggregation: not hit; this is a fused reduction.
- Point 17, redundant boundary operations: not hit; the R6 candidate has no
  boundary masks or redundant `where`/mask arithmetic.
- Point 18, kernel splitting: no new viable group. Every case has the same
  reduce-last N=2048 and dtype contract; the only small/large grouping was R7
  and was rejected by authoritative measurements.
- Points 19-29 are not applicable: there is no Cube, dot, gather, workspace,
  atomic scatter, default-output fill, interpolation, pooling, matmul, or CV
  pipeline in this pure Vector RMSNorm.

### R8 — optimization point 30: explicit A5 multibuffer (`bc21966`, rejected)

- Latest R6 IR is analyzed at TTIR, TTAdapter, and final BishengIR. It contains
  the expected three loads/two stores and a single row pipeline. TTAdapter has
  `tt.num_stages=2`, but final IR exposes no automatic multibuffer annotation.
- A5 documentation states that 910_95/950 defaults `multibuffer=False`.
  Profiling corroborates a memory bottleneck: at M16384 per-AIV MTE2 is
  253.224 us versus 109.326 us Vector, while R6 has already removed the only
  redundant output write.
- Unique optimization point: pass only `multibuffer=True` to the candidate
  launch so the compiler can overlap the next row's GM load with current-row
  Vector reduction/store. Baseline stays frozen.
- Kernel source, `tl.range(num_stages=2)`, row partition, grid, BLOCK, BF16
  rounding point, FP32 reduction order, and both stores are unchanged.
- This compiler option is shape-independent. All M values remain runtime
  values in `do_not_specialize`; the new decode M12/24/40/48/56 cases expand
  validation coverage without creating shape-derived constexpr parameters.
- A5 correctness: all 36 records PASS, including added M12/24/40/48/56.
- Fresh final BishengIR SHA256 is exactly identical to R6
  (`1cabcc08...10b2`), proving that this compile path discards or fully
  canonicalizes the option. TTIR/TTAdapter metadata hashes change, but final
  device code does not expose a multibuffer transformation.
- Direct candidate p50 versus the preceding unchanged-path run is M4096
  67.861 -> 70.042 us, M8192 154.673 -> 155.762 us, M9616 183.398 ->
  184.440 us, M16361 341.370 -> 343.899 us, and M16384 349.286 ->
  348.508 us. There is no material improvement; M4096 regresses 3.2%.
- Decision: rejected. Remove `multibuffer=True` and keep the retained R6
  kernel plus the expanded decode coverage.
- `hit_optimization_point: 30 (IR-guided revisit of multibuffer)`
- `ir_has_more_suggestions: true (pure-Vector flatten option remains untested)`

### R9 — optimization point 30: explicit pure-Vector flatten (`21f8382`, rejected)

- A5 compiler guidance recommends `enable_flatten=True` for pure Vector
  kernels. The candidate is a row loop containing only GM loads, Vector
  arithmetic/reduction, and GM stores; it has no Cube or CV synchronization.
- Unique optimization point: replace the rejected compile option with only
  `enable_flatten=True` on the candidate launch. No multibuffer option remains.
- All R6 JIT source, runtime `rows`, grid, arithmetic, and output stores stay
  unchanged. The option is constant across all shapes and cannot create an
  M-derived JIT key.
- Expected IR effect: flatten/canonicalize the per-program row loop so the
  backend may simplify scheduling and reduce loop-control overhead while
  preserving one independent RMS reduction per row.
- A5 correctness: all 36 records PASS.
- TTIR, TTAdapter, and final BishengIR hashes are all exactly identical to R8;
  final device code again has SHA256 `1cabcc08...10b2`. The option produces no
  compiler transformation for this kernel/toolchain.
- Candidate Prefill p50 is 67.098/154.388/183.456/343.715/350.758 us. Relative
  to the retained R6 control run, all differences are about +/-1%; no shape
  clears the 3% retention threshold. Decode measurements are launch-overhead
  dominated and mixed, while generated device code is identical.
- Decision: rejected. Remove `enable_flatten=True`; retain R6 two-output source
  and the expanded M12/24/40/48/56 decode validation matrix.
- `hit_optimization_point: 30 (IR-guided pure-Vector flatten)`
- `ir_has_more_suggestions: false`

### Final retained candidate

- R6 is the retained implementation: one dynamic-M, physical-AIV, balanced
  contiguous-row kernel producing only consumed BF16 normalized hidden and
  FP32 normalized residual outputs.
- No shape/token-derived value is constexpr. `rows` remains covered by
  `do_not_specialize`; N=2048 and BLOCK=2048 are fixed model-contract values.
- All ordered latency-skill points and the IR-guided follow-up suggestions have
  been checked. Rejected branches are removed from executable source.

### R10 — IR-guided 4-row Prefill tile (`3080331`, retained)

- The 30% target reopens IR iteration. R6 executes about 293 row-loop
  iterations per AIV at M16384, issuing separate hidden/residual loads and
  FP32/BF16 stores for every row. MTE2 remains the dominant pipeline.
- Unique optimization point: for Prefill M>=4096, group four independent rows
  into one regular `[4,2048]` tile. This cuts row-loop and DMA issue count to
  one quarter and matches the A5 multi-row tiling guidance.
- Decode keeps the retained R6 kernel byte-for-byte. The Prefill grid is
  `min(ceil(M/4), physical_AIV)` and each AIV receives contiguous row blocks.
- M remains a runtime value in `do_not_specialize`; `BLOCK_ROWS=4` and N=2048
  are fixed tiling/model constants. The sole row mask protects the final tile.
- Conservative UB estimate is below the A5 248 KiB budget with multibuffer
  disabled. No arithmetic order changes within a row: FP32 residual add, BF16
  rounding, FP32 reduction/rsqrt/gamma, and both output dtypes are preserved.
- `hit_optimization_point: 30 (IR-guided multi-row tiling)`
- A5 correctness: all 36 records PASS.
- Authoritative Prefill speedups are M4096 1.4464x, M8192 1.4775x,
  M9616 1.4165x, M16361 1.3305x, and M16384 1.3293x. Every Prefill case
  clears the per-shape 30% target.
- At M16384, mean per-AIV MTE2 instruction count falls 1758 -> 442 and MTE3
  586 -> 147, almost exactly one quarter. Scalar time falls 36.9 -> 10.7 us;
  Task Duration falls from the R6 range near 350 us to 283.4 us.
- Decision: retained. The four-row kernel is now the protected Prefill path.
- `ir_has_more_suggestions: true (Decode still misses the 30% target)`

### R10 compile fix (`8baf663` failure)

- Remote compilation fails before execution: reducing `[4,2048]` on the last
  axis returns `[4]`; the one-row helper's implicit multiply aligns that 4 to
  the 2048 tail dimension and reports incompatible dimensions.
- Fix only broadcasting: use a 2D helper with `inv_rms[:, None]` and
  `gamma[None, :]`. Per-row FP32 reduction, rsqrt, multiply order, dtypes,
  tiling, masks, and launch grid are unchanged.

### R11 — Decode-only A5 SIMT path (`154f4cc`, rejected)

- R10 Prefill remains byte-for-byte unchanged. Only the one-row Decode
  candidate launch uses `compile_mode="simt_only"`.
- Motivation from M1 msprof: Task is 3.02 us while AIV active is 1.73 us;
  Scalar is 0.556 us and Vector wait ratio is 61.6%. The workload is too small
  to fill 56 SIMD AIVs and is not HBM-bandwidth-bound.
- SIMT is an A5/950-specific alternative intended to reduce control and tiny
  vector-task overhead. The tensor arithmetic, grid, runtime rows, exact
  N=2048, and two outputs are unchanged.
- Compile mode is fixed for the named Decode kernel and independent of M;
  `rows` remains in `do_not_specialize`.
- A5 correctness passes, but every Decode shape regresses severely. Candidate
  Task Duration grows from 4.204 us at M1 to 8.738 us at M128; speedups range
  only 0.578x to 0.748x.
- Prefill controls remain at 1.325x to 1.531x, proving the protected R10 path
  is unaffected.
- Decision: reject and remove `compile_mode="simt_only"`.
- `hit_optimization_point: 30 (A5 Decode SIMT specialization)`
- `ir_has_more_suggestions: true (multi-row Decode group remains)`

### R12 — two-row Decode tile for M40-128 (`3b55ecf`, retained)

- Unique optimization point: M40/48/56/64/128 use the retained multi-row
  kernel with `BLOCK_ROWS=2`; M1-32 return to the R6 one-row SIMD kernel.
- Grid remains at 20/24/28/32/56 programs respectively, while load/store and
  loop issue count per program is aggregated across two contiguous rows.
- Prefill continues to use the same multi-row source with BLOCK_ROWS=4 and is
  otherwise unchanged. Runtime M stays in `do_not_specialize`; BLOCK_ROWS is a
  fixed tiling choice with only two stable compiled variants.
- A5 correctness passes. Decode speedups are M40 1.0847x, M48 1.1424x,
  M56 1.1203x, M64 1.1932x, and M128 1.1582x. Every targeted case clears
  the 3% retention threshold, though none yet reaches 1.30x.
- Prefill controls remain 1.3287x to 1.4622x.
- Decision: retain two-row tiling for M40-128.
- `hit_optimization_point: 30 (Decode multi-row tiling)`
- `ir_has_more_suggestions: true (per-tile boundary mask remains)`

### R13 — no-mask full multi-row blocks plus one runtime tail (`178ca3f`, rejected)

- Unique optimization point: all complete BLOCK_ROWS tiles use unmasked 2D
  loads/stores. Only the final incomplete tile, when runtime `tail_start < M`,
  executes one masked path on the last program.
- M4096/8192/9616/16384 and all two-row Decode cases are exactly divisible,
  so their hot loops contain no boundary mask. M16361 executes 4090 full
  four-row blocks plus one masked one-row tail.
- Runtime M and tail existence are ordinary scalar values, not constexpr or
  JIT keys. There is one fixed multi-row kernel per BLOCK_ROWS tiling variant;
  no per-shape compilation is introduced.
- Arithmetic order and output dtypes are unchanged.
- A5 correctness passes. Prefill speedups are only 1.4783x/1.4658x/1.4164x/
  1.3135x/1.3402x; no case reaches 1.50x and M16361 worsens to 286.0 us.
- Decode is mixed: M56 and M128 rise to 1.201x and 1.259x, but M64 falls to
  1.097x. This strategy may be revisited as a Decode-only named path.
- Decision: reject for the shared multi-row kernel and restore R12's masked
  loop before changing Prefill tiling.
- `hit_optimization_point: 30 (IR-guided redundant boundary removal)`
- `ir_has_more_suggestions: true (larger Prefill tile remains)`

### R14 — eight-row Prefill tile (`642c3dd`, rejected)

- Unique optimization point from retained R12: Prefill BLOCK_ROWS changes
  from 4 to 8. Decode remains BLOCK_ROWS=2 with the retained masked loop.
- Goal: halve Prefill loop/DMA issue count again and increase each regular 2D
  transfer. Runtime M, masks, arithmetic, grid cap, and output contract remain
  unchanged.
- A5 UB risk is explicitly tested. If the compiler reports overflow or any
  precision failure, the tiling is rejected without relaxing the contract.
- A5 compilation fails in PlanMemory: the kernel requires 3,211,520 bits of
  UB while Ascend950PR_957b exposes 2,031,616 bits. The failed final IR marks
  the large hidden/residual/output buffers with `hivm.multi_buffer = 2`.
- Decision: eight rows cannot be retained with the two-stage row loop.
- `hit_optimization_point: 30 (IR-guided larger multi-row tile)`
- `ir_has_more_suggestions: true (remove Prefill-only double buffering)`

### R15 — eight-row Prefill tile with a single pipeline stage (`6e4d2b2`, rejected)

- Unique optimization point from failed R14: only the Prefill multi-row loop
  changes from `num_stages=2` to `num_stages=1`. The eight-row DMA tile stays
  fixed; Decode keeps BLOCK_ROWS=2 and `num_stages=2`.
- Goal: remove the IR-observed double UB buffers so the eight-row tile fits in
  2,031,616 bits. This may reduce load/compute/store overlap, but retains the
  halved DMA/loop issue count relative to four-row R12.
- `NUM_STAGES` is a stable kernel-strategy constexpr, not a request-derived
  value. Runtime M remains in `do_not_specialize`; arithmetic, masks, grid,
  output dtypes, and all shape behavior are unchanged.
- A5 fails with exactly the same 3,211,520-bit UB requirement. Final IR still
  marks every large buffer `hivm.multi_buffer = 2`; on this NPU backend,
  `tl.range(num_stages=1)` does not control the automatic multibuffer pass.
- Decision: reject and remove the ineffective `NUM_STAGES` meta-parameter.
- `hit_optimization_point: 18/30 (pipeline depth for UB-safe larger tiling)`
- `ir_has_more_suggestions: true (use the NPU multibuffer compile option)`

### R16 — explicitly disable multibuffer for eight-row Prefill (`1afd907`, rejected)

- Unique effective optimization point from R14: launch only the eight-row
  Prefill variant with the NPU compile option `multibuffer=False`. Decode
  remains BLOCK_ROWS=2 with its existing/default multibuffer behavior.
- This directly controls the A5 backend option that produced five observed
  `hivm.multi_buffer = 2` buffers. Removing the ineffective R15 NUM_STAGES
  parameter is behavior-neutral cleanup supported by its identical failed IR.
- Runtime M stays in `do_not_specialize`; `multibuffer=False` belongs to one
  stable named Prefill strategy and does not vary with request shape.
- Arithmetic, masks, grid, row mapping, and output contract are unchanged.
- A5 correctness passes all 36 records and UB compilation succeeds, proving
  the launch option controls the pass. However Prefill speedups fall to
  1.2172x/1.3272x/1.3264x/1.2993x/1.2935x, below retained R12/R10.
- At M16384 the candidate uses only about 219--225 MTE2 and 73--75 MTE3
  instructions per AIV, but without multibuffer MTE2 and MTE3 each occupy
  roughly half the AIV timeline. The lost overlap outweighs larger DMA tiles.
- Decision: reject; restore four rows and the default A5 multibuffer path.
- `hit_optimization_point: 18/30 (UB-safe large tile via compile option)`
- `ir_has_more_suggestions: true (dual-output multiple-consumer fusion)`

### R17 — fuse the FP32 output's two consumers (`6df767a`, rejected)

- Unique effective optimization point from retained R12: enable
  `hfusion_enable_multiple_consumer_fusion=True` only for the four-row Prefill
  variant. BLOCK_ROWS returns to 4 and automatic multibuffer remains enabled.
- The normalized FP32 tile has two consumers: direct FP32 store and BF16 cast
  followed by BF16 store. R14/R16 final IR materializes a full FP32 output UB
  buffer before these branches; the HFusion option is specifically intended
  to fuse multiple-consumer graphs and defaults to false.
- Goal: reduce intermediate UB traffic/lifetime and synchronization while
  preserving the four-row load/compute/store overlap.
- The option is one stable Prefill compile strategy. Runtime M remains in
  `do_not_specialize`; no request-derived constexpr or dispatch is added.
- A5 correctness passes, but TTIR, TTAdapter, and final-IR SHA256 values are
  byte-for-byte identical to retained R12. Candidate Prefill times are within
  normal run variation (48.491/116.864/146.092/280.569/285.454 us), so the
  compiler option did not transform this graph and adds no structural gain.
- Decision: reject and remove the ineffective option.
- `hit_optimization_point: 30 (IR-guided multiple-consumer fusion)`
- `ir_has_more_suggestions: true (AutoVectorizeV2 on the 2D apply chain)`

### R18 — AutoVectorizeV2 for four-row Prefill (`083e081`, rejected)

- Unique optimization point from retained R12: enable only
  `enable_auto_vectorize_v2=True` for the fixed four-row Prefill variant.
- Motivation: the retained profile still spends substantial AIV time in the
  vector reduction/apply/cast chain after DMA issue count was reduced. V2 can
  alter vector fusion/layout independently of the rejected multi-consumer
  pass, and its effect is verified by fresh IR hashes and msprof counters.
- Runtime M, masks, four-row tiling, multibuffer, arithmetic order, and output
  dtypes are unchanged. The option is not request-derived and cannot create
  per-M compiled variants.
- A5 correctness passes, but all three IR hashes are again exactly identical
  to R12/R17. Only M4096 happens to measure 1.548x in this run; the other four
  remain 1.456x/1.424x/1.333x/1.333x, demonstrating measurement variation
  rather than a transformed pipeline.
- Decision: reject; stop testing adjacent no-op compiler toggles.
- `hit_optimization_point: 30 (IR-guided vector pipeline optimization)`
- `ir_has_more_suggestions: true (selective manual prefetch for eight rows)`

### R19 — loop-carried BF16-hidden prefetch (`30c0780`, rejected implementation)

- Unique optimization point from R16's working eight-row single-buffer path:
  manually preload only the next tile's BF16 hidden tensor. The current and
  next hidden tiles consume 32 KiB each; residual, FP32 norm input/output, and
  BF16 output remain single-buffered under `multibuffer=False`.
- The loop has a prologue, an unconditional next-tile load for every non-final
  iteration, and one final epilogue. It does not issue a duplicate last-tile
  prefetch and contains no data-dependent prefetch branch.
- Goal: preserve R16's roughly 219--225 MTE2 and 73--75 MTE3 instructions at
  M16384 while overlapping part of next MTE2 with current reduction/stores.
- `PREFETCH_HIDDEN=True/False` is a fixed algorithm strategy (Prefill/Decode),
  not a request value. Runtime M remains in `do_not_specialize`, and all masks
  and loop bounds remain dynamic. Decode retains its R12 two-row path.
- Per-row arithmetic and both output dtypes are unchanged.
- Remote compilation aborts in TritonToLinalg BlockPtrAnalysis while rewriting
  the `scf.yield` for the loop-carried 2D tensor: `attribute strides should be
  ones`. This is a frontend limitation, not UB or a numeric failure.
- Decision: reject this implementation; retain the selective-prefetch idea
  without carrying tensors across loop iterations.
- `hit_optimization_point: 18/30 (manual selective double buffering)`
- `ir_has_more_suggestions: true (pair two tiles inside each iteration)`

### R20 — paired eight-row blocks with selective hidden overlap (`8650f6a`, rejected)

- Unique implementation correction from R19: partition global row blocks into
  runtime pairs. Each loop iteration loads both `[8,2048]` BF16 hidden tiles,
  then computes/stores the first and second tile sequentially.
- No tensor is carried by `scf.yield`, so the failing BlockPtrAnalysis path is
  removed. Only two BF16 hidden tiles coexist; residual and FP32 intermediates
  are created sequentially for one eight-row tile at a time.
- `num_block_pairs`, pair ranges, M, and both masks are runtime values. The
  sole compile-time strategy constants remain fixed BLOCK_ROWS=8 and the
  Prefill-only algorithm flag; no request shape enters the JIT key.
- Arithmetic and output contracts remain identical. Decode is unchanged.
- A5 reaches PlanMemory but requires 2,621,696 bits (about 320 KiB) versus
  2,031,616 bits available. Final IR allocates distinct FP32/BF16 compute and
  output buffers for both sequential tiles instead of reusing the first set.
- Decision: reject; an extra 8-row BF16 tile cannot coexist with the compiler's
  generated temporaries under the exact dual-output contract.
- `hit_optimization_point: 18/30 (paired manual selective buffering)`
- `ir_has_more_suggestions: true (split the reduction's column axis)`

### R21 — eight rows with two fixed 1024-column reduction halves (`9695d1a`, rejected)

- Unique algorithmic point: a dedicated Prefill kernel keeps BLOCK_ROWS=8 but
  splits fixed N=2048 into two 1024-column halves. Each half performs the same
  FP32 residual add followed by BF16 rounding and a FP32 partial square sum;
  the two partial sums are added before rsqrt.
- The apply phase reuses each half's rounded FP32 values and writes the exact
  FP32/BF16 dual outputs. The binary reduction tree is expressed as two 1024
  reductions plus one add, matching the natural top level of a 2048 reduction;
  full reference accuracy remains mandatory.
- Goal: retain eight-row DMA while reducing the live width of FP32 elementwise
  temporaries from 2048 to 1024. Automatic multibuffer stays disabled for this
  large-tile kernel; explicit half ordering may expose intra-tile MTE/Vector
  overlap without duplicating a complete tile.
- Runtime rows, row-block ranges, and masks remain dynamic and rows stays in
  `do_not_specialize`. BLOCK_ROWS=8, BLOCK_COLS=1024, and N=2048 are fixed
  model/tiling constants, never request-derived values.
- Decode returns byte-for-byte to the retained R12 two-row generic kernel.
- A5 correctness passes all 36 records, but Prefill speedups are only
  1.3626x/1.4002x/1.3918x/1.3041x/1.3265x. Every shape is slower than the
  retained four-row path, so the smaller FP32 working width does not recover
  the overlap lost by disabling automatic multibuffer.
- Decision: reject and remove the dedicated split-column kernel.
- `hit_optimization_point: 18/30 (column tiling and partial reduction)`
- `ir_has_more_suggestions: false for the current Prefill optimization phase`

### Prefill freeze after R21

- Per user direction, stop Prefill optimization when R21 shows no clear gain.
- Restore the verified R12 four-row implementation. R12, R17, and R18 have
  identical TTIR/TTAdapter/final-IR hashes; the later compile switches were
  proven no-ops. This path keeps runtime M in `do_not_specialize`, one stable
  kernel strategy, full dynamic tail masking, and 36/36 reference accuracy.
- The retained representative R12 task times are 48.678/118.133/146.135/
  282.868/283.262 us for M4096/8192/9616/16361/16384.
- Further rounds target Decode only; Prefill is a protected control path.

### R22 — Decode-only unmasked full two-row blocks (`5a9c0e9`, rejected)

- Prefill remains byte-for-byte identical to frozen R12.
- Unique Decode optimization point: even runtime M>=48 uses a named full-block
  kernel with BLOCK_ROWS=2 and no load/store boundary masks. M40, odd M, and
  M1--32 retain the verified masked/one-row paths.
- Historical R13 absolute candidate times improved over R12 by about 5% at
  M48, 10% at M56, and 11% at M128. M40 improved only 1% and is deliberately
  excluded. M64 is remeasured because its earlier 4% regression may be noise.
- `rows` remains in `do_not_specialize` in both named kernels. The Python
  parity/range dispatch selects one stable algorithm and never passes M as a
  constexpr, so M48/56/64/128 share one compiled full-block variant.
- Per-row arithmetic, grid, BF16 output, and FP32 output are unchanged.
- `hit_optimization_point: 30 (Decode boundary-mask elimination)`

- A5 correctness passes all 36 checks, and the frozen Prefill controls remain
  stable at 48.240/117.416/145.998/282.973/281.186 us.
- Absolute candidate task times versus the immediately preceding R12 restore
  are M48 3.449 -> 3.679 us, M56 3.519 -> 3.634 us, M64 3.565 ->
  3.594 us, and M128 4.454 -> 4.481 us. Removing the masks produces no new
  material gain and regresses M48/M56.
- Decision: reject. Remove the named full-block kernel and restore the masked
  R12 two-row implementation before the next Decode-only experiment.

### R23 — balanced four-row Decode tile for M128 (`a3ebb37`, rejected)

- Prefill remains byte-for-byte identical to frozen R12. Decode M1--64 also
  returns to the retained R12 paths.
- R22 `PipeUtilization.csv` shows why M128 is distinct: 64 two-row tiles are
  partitioned over 56 programs, so exactly eight AIVs execute two loop
  iterations. Those eight AIVs take roughly 3.8 us and determine the task,
  while the one-iteration AIVs generally take about 2.2--3.1 us.
- Unique optimization point: M128 reuses the already validated
  `BLOCK_ROWS=4` multi-row variant with 32 programs. Every program executes
  one regular `[4, 2048]` tile, removing the 56-to-64 imbalance and combining
  the critical AIVs' two double-row DMA/reduction iterations into one.
- This does not add a JIT variant: Prefill already uses the same named kernel
  with `BLOCK_ROWS=4`; runtime `rows` remains in `do_not_specialize` and only
  the launch grid changes. No arithmetic, reduction order within a row, dtype,
  mask contract, or output changes.
- `hit_optimization_point: 30 (Decode four-row tiling and balanced grid)`

- A5 correctness passes all 36 checks. Frozen Prefill controls remain in their
  retained range, including M16384 at 281.866 us.
- M128 regresses from the verified R12 absolute candidate time 4.454 us to
  5.335 us (about 19.8% slower), and is also slower than its same-run baseline
  at 0.9301x. Combining two double-row iterations into one four-row tile does
  not compensate for halving the active program count from 56 to 32.
- Decision: reject. Restore R12 exactly. Do not test the still smaller M64 or
  M48/M56 with four-row tiling: they would expose only 16/12/14 programs and
  have an even weaker parallelism/aggregation tradeoff.

### Final freeze after Decode recheck

- Prefill: retain R12 `BLOCK_ROWS=4` for M>=4096.
- Decode: retain R12 one-row path for M1--32 and `BLOCK_ROWS=2` for M40--128.
- R22 proves boundary-mask removal has no repeatable absolute-time benefit;
  R23 proves four-row Decode aggregation loses too much parallelism. Remaining
  small-M task time is dominated by the roughly 3 us launch/task envelope, not
  removable tensor traffic, so no remaining skill point has a justified >=3%
  expectation without changing the operator boundary or using graph fusion.
- `ir_has_more_suggestions: pending accuracy and msprof op`
